IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `tasap-dates`

-- Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) -- the claims machinery around an estate: ss 28, 29, 43(1), Part VII
-- (ss 54 to 61) and s 65.
--
-- SEAT: the individual with a claim on a dead person's estate: a creditor or other person with a claim against the
-- estate (ss 29, 54 to 61), a beneficiary waiting for distribution (s 43(1)), a person defrauded by the hiding of a will
-- (s 65), and a person who paid money or handed property to the executor or administrator (s 28). The personal
-- representative's own duties appear only as what they mean for that person. The Court, the judge and the Registrar get
-- no seat.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list).
--
-- Edition: Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas), No. 38 of 1935, consolidated version published 2024-07-18 and
-- current at point in time 2026-11-06, deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/act-1935-038.txt. The amount
-- "prescribed ... for the purpose of section 20A(1) of the Public Trustee Act 1930" (s 54(10)) is set outside the Act
-- and not deposited: it is asked as a fact.
--
-- (c) State of Tasmania. Quoted under CC BY 4.0 from an unofficial plain-text copy (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Based on
-- material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05
-- (NOTES.md): "(the text's no)" marks the text's own no; "NOTHING-kind" marks where the text says nothing more.

§ `Claims on a dead person's estate`

§§ `s 28 -- Payments made to the executor or administrator in good faith`

-- Situation: only arises if you paid money or handed property over under a grant of probate or administration.
-- s 28(1): "Every person making or permitting to be made any payment or disposition in good faith under a
-- representation shall be indemnified and protected in so doing, notwithstanding any defect or circumstance whatsoever
-- affecting the validity of the representation."
-- s 28(2): "Where a representation is revoked, all payments and dispositions made in good faith to a personal
-- representative under the representation before the revocation thereof are a valid discharge of the person making the
-- same".
-- A payment not in good faith, or not under a grant, is outside s 28's own words (NOTHING-kind). A payment made after
-- the grant was revoked is read as no longer "under a representation" (NOTHING-kind, fork F10).

DECLARE `Am I protected for the payment` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you are indemnified and protected for a payment or disposition made in good faith under the grant, whatever defect affects its validity (s 28(1))`
    `yes: the grant has been revoked, but a payment or disposition made in good faith to the personal representative before the revocation is a valid discharge, and you are protected (s 28(1) and (2))`
    `s 28 gives no protection on these facts: the payment or disposition was made after the grant was revoked (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 28 gives no protection on these facts: the payment or disposition was not made in good faith under a grant of probate or administration (NOTHING-kind)`

@export I paid money or handed property over under a grant of probate or letters of administration: am I protected if the grant turns out to be defective or is revoked (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 28)? Yes, if you acted in good faith under the grant; a payment in good faith to the executor or administrator before the grant was revoked is a valid discharge.
GIVEN `under a grant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the payment or disposition under a grant of probate or letters of administration (for example, paying a debt you owed the dead person to their executor)
      `in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you acted in good faith (honestly, without knowing of a problem with the grant)
      `to the personal representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payment or disposition was made to the executor or administrator
      `revoked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the grant has since been revoked
      `before the revocation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the grant has been revoked: whether you made the payment or disposition before it was revoked (if not revoked, it is not used)
GIVETH A `Am I protected for the payment`
`s 28 -- am I protected for a payment under the grant` `under a grant` `in good faith` `to the personal representative` `revoked` `before the revocation` MEANS
    IF NOT (`under a grant` AND `in good faith`)
    THEN `s 28 gives no protection on these facts: the payment or disposition was not made in good faith under a grant of probate or administration (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF NOT `revoked`
         THEN `yes: you are indemnified and protected for a payment or disposition made in good faith under the grant, whatever defect affects its validity (s 28(1))`
         ELSE IF NOT `before the revocation`
              THEN `s 28 gives no protection on these facts: the payment or disposition was made after the grant was revoked (NOTHING-kind)`
              ELSE IF `to the personal representative`
                   THEN `yes: the grant has been revoked, but a payment or disposition made in good faith to the personal representative before the revocation is a valid discharge, and you are protected (s 28(1) and (2))`
                   ELSE `yes: you are indemnified and protected for a payment or disposition made in good faith under the grant, whatever defect affects its validity (s 28(1))`

§§ `s 29 -- A person who took the estate's property: executor in their own wrong`

-- s 29: "If any person, to the defrauding of creditors or without full valuable consideration, obtains, receives, or
-- holds any real or personal estate of a deceased person, or effects the release of any debt or liability due to the
-- estate of the deceased, he shall be charged as executor in his own wrong to the extent of the real and personal
-- estate received or coming into his hands, or the debt or liability released, after deducting -- (a) any debt for
-- valuable consideration and without fraud due to him from the deceased person at the time of his death; and (b) any
-- payment made by him which might properly be made by a personal representative."
-- The "if" failing is the text's no. The deductions are taken off the value; the charge does not go below 0 (fork F11).

@export Can the person who took or kept property of the dead person's estate (or had a debt to the estate released), without paying full value for it or to defraud the creditors, be made to answer for it as if they were the executor (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 29)? Yes: they are charged as "executor in their own wrong".
GIVEN `took estate property or had a debt released` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person obtained, received or holds property of the dead person's estate, or brought about the release of a debt or liability owed to the estate
      `to defraud creditors` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did so to the defrauding of the dead person's creditors
      `full value given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they gave full valuable consideration (paid the full value) for it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 29 -- is the person charged as executor in their own wrong` `took estate property or had a debt released` `to defraud creditors` `full value given` IF
        `took estate property or had a debt released`
    AND (    `to defraud creditors`
         OR  NOT `full value given`)

@export How much can the person who took the dead person's property (or had a debt to the estate released) be made to answer for as executor in their own wrong (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 29)? The value received, or the debt released, less any honest debt the dead person owed them for value and any payments they made that an executor could properly have made. 0 where s 29 does not charge them.
GIVEN `took estate property or had a debt released` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person obtained, received or holds property of the dead person's estate, or brought about the release of a debt or liability owed to the estate
      `to defraud creditors` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did so to the defrauding of the dead person's creditors
      `full value given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they gave full valuable consideration (paid the full value) for it
      `value received` IS A NUMBER @desc The value (in dollars) of the estate's property that came into their hands, plus the amount of any debt or liability released
      `debt owed to them` IS A NUMBER @desc Any debt (in dollars) the dead person owed them at the death, for valuable consideration and without fraud
      `proper payments` IS A NUMBER @desc Payments (in dollars) they made that an executor or administrator could properly have made (for example the funeral, or the dead person's debts)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 29 -- how much is the person charged` `took estate property or had a debt released` `to defraud creditors` `full value given` `value received` `debt owed to them` `proper payments` MEANS
    IF `s 29 -- is the person charged as executor in their own wrong` `took estate property or had a debt released` `to defraud creditors` `full value given`
    THEN IF `the balance` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the balance`
    ELSE 0
    WHERE
        `the balance` MEANS `value received` MINUS (`debt owed to them` PLUS `proper payments`)

§§ `s 43(1) -- The executor's year`

-- s 43(1): "Subject to the foregoing provisions of this Act, a personal representative is not bound to distribute the
-- estate of the deceased before the expiration of one year from the death." A flat negative: before the year has
-- expired, the text's no. After it, s 43(1) says nothing about when distribution must happen (NOTHING-kind). The year
-- expires at the end of the anniversary of the death (fork F7).

DECLARE `Can I insist on distribution yet` IS ONE OF
    `no: the executor or administrator is not bound to distribute the estate before one year from the death has expired (s 43(1), the text's no)`
    `s 43(1) no longer stands in the way: one year from the death has expired; the Act does not itself fix a later day by which the estate must be distributed (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I, as a beneficiary or next of kin, insist that the executor or administrator distribute the estate now (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 43(1))? Not before one year from the death has expired: until then they are not bound to distribute.
GIVEN `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Can I insist on distribution yet`
`s 43(1) -- can I insist on distribution yet` `died on` `today` MEANS
    IF `the year's last day` `falls before` `today`
    THEN `s 43(1) no longer stands in the way: one year from the death has expired; the Act does not itself fix a later day by which the estate must be distributed (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE `no: the executor or administrator is not bound to distribute the estate before one year from the death has expired (s 43(1), the text's no)`
    WHERE
        `the year's last day` MEANS `months after` 12 `died on`

§§ `s 54(7) -- The closing day in a notice for claims`

-- Situation: only arises if the executor or administrator has advertised for claims against the estate.
-- s 54(6): the closing day "shall be the same in every such advertisement relating to such estate".
-- s 54(7): "The day to be specified as aforesaid shall, if the advertisements are published -- (a) only in this State,
-- be not less than one nor more than two; (b) in any other State or New Zealand, be not less than two nor more than 4;
-- (c) in London, be not less than 4 nor more than 8 -- months after the last of such publications."
-- Where advertisements went both interstate and to London, the London range is taken (fork F12). Outside the range
-- the text's no. The Act does not say what follows from a closing day outside the range (finding 5).

DECLARE `Where the notices were published` IS ONE OF
    `only in Tasmania`
    `also in another State or in New Zealand, but not in London`
    `also in London (the London Gazette)`

DECLARE `Is the closing day one the Act allows` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the closing day is within the range s 54(7) allows`
    `no: the closing day is earlier than s 54(7) allows (the text's no)`
    `no: the closing day is later than s 54(7) allows (the text's no)`

@export Is the closing day for claims in the executor's or administrator's notice one the Act allows (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 54(7))? It must be 1 to 2 months after the last publication where the notices appeared only in Tasmania, 2 to 4 months where they also appeared in another State or New Zealand, and 4 to 8 months where they also appeared in London.
GIVEN `where published` IS A `Where the notices were published` @desc Where the notices for claims were published
      `last published on` IS A DATE @desc The day the last of the notices was published
      `closing day` IS A DATE @desc The day named in the notices as the last day for claims to be sent to the Registrar
GIVETH A `Is the closing day one the Act allows`
`s 54(7) -- is the closing day for claims one the Act allows` `where published` `last published on` `closing day` MEANS
    IF `closing day` `falls before` (`months after` `the fewest months` `last published on`)
    THEN `no: the closing day is earlier than s 54(7) allows (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF (`months after` `the most months` `last published on`) `falls before` `closing day`
         THEN `no: the closing day is later than s 54(7) allows (the text's no)`
         ELSE `yes: the closing day is within the range s 54(7) allows`
    WHERE
        `the fewest months` MEANS
            IF `where published` EQUALS `only in Tasmania`
            THEN 1
            ELSE IF `where published` EQUALS `also in another State or in New Zealand, but not in London`
                 THEN 2
                 ELSE 4
        `the most months` MEANS
            IF `where published` EQUALS `only in Tasmania`
            THEN 2
            ELSE IF `where published` EQUALS `also in another State or in New Zealand, but not in London`
                 THEN 4
                 ELSE 8

§§ `ss 55 to 61 -- What happens to a claim after the closing day`

-- Situation: only arises if the executor or administrator has advertised for claims against the estate.
-- s 55: after the closing day the personal representative "shall be at liberty to pay and distribute the assets ...
-- so far as respects the claims of which he then has notice, whether as a result of such claims being filed ... or
-- otherwise", after "satisfying, or retaining sufficient to satisfy" them.
-- s 56(3): on filing the s 56 account, the personal representative is "released from all claims and demands ... in
-- respect of the assets so paid or distributed, except at the suit or on the behalf of persons who have filed claims on
-- or before the day specified ..., or of whose claims [he] has otherwise then had notice, and except also at the suit
-- or on behalf of persons having claims against the estate who are resident in any other State or New Zealand, where
-- the advertisement has not been published as aforesaid, and any such person resident elsewhere than in any other
-- State or New Zealand where the advertisement has not been published in London as provided by section 54."
-- s 54(10): s 54(4) (the interstate, New Zealand and London notices) does not apply where the net value of the estate
-- does not exceed the amount prescribed for s 20A(1) of the Public Trustee Act 1930. For such an estate the s 56(3)
-- exception for claimants living outside Tasmania is taken not to arise (fork F13, finding 4).
-- s 57: "nothing herein contained shall prevent any legatee, next-of-kin, or other person, having any claim against the
-- estate, from taking such proceedings as he might have taken if this Act had not been passed", save as respects the
-- release. s 60: nothing in Part VII affects a claimant's rights against a legatee or next-of-kin who has received
-- assets. s 61: further assets are dealt with in the same way. s 59: the release stands except as to errors or
-- omissions in the account (noted, not asked).

DECLARE `Where I lived at the death` IS ONE OF
    `in Tasmania`
    `in another State or territory, or in New Zealand`
    `outside Australia and New Zealand`

DECLARE `What is left of my claim` IS ONE OF
    `Part VII does not touch your claim: the executor or administrator has not advertised for claims (NOTHING-kind); you may take the proceedings you otherwise could (s 57)`
    `your claim is kept: the executor or administrator must satisfy it, or keep back enough to satisfy it, before distributing (s 55), and is not released from it when the account is filed (s 56(3))`
    `your claim is kept against the executor or administrator: you lived outside Tasmania and the notice was not published where s 54(4) requires, so the s 56(3) release does not reach you`
    `the executor or administrator is released from your claim as to the assets paid or distributed (s 56(3)); you may still follow those assets into the hands of the beneficiaries or next of kin who received them (s 60), and assets not yet distributed still answer your claim (s 57)`
    `the executor or administrator may now distribute without regard to your claim (s 55), and will be released as to what is distributed once the account is filed (s 56(3)); you may still follow assets into the hands of the beneficiaries or next of kin who received them (s 60)`

@export I have a claim against a dead person's estate and the executor or administrator advertised for claims: what happens to my claim if I did not send it to the Registrar by the closing day (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) ss 54 to 61)? A claim sent in time, or one the executor otherwise knew of, must be met or provided for; otherwise the executor may distribute and is released as to what was distributed, but you can still follow the assets to those who received them.
GIVEN `advertised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor or administrator has advertised for claims against the estate (in the Gazette and newspapers, naming a closing day)
      `sent by the closing day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you sent your claim in writing to the Registrar on or before the closing day
      `executor otherwise knew` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor or administrator otherwise knew of your claim (before paying out, or when filing their account)
      `where I lived` IS A `Where I lived at the death` @desc Where you lived when the person died
      `notice published where I lived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you lived outside Tasmania: whether a notice for claims was published in a newspaper where you lived (another State or New Zealand), or in the London Gazette (elsewhere)
      `small estate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the net value of the estate is not more than the amount prescribed for section 20A(1) of the Public Trustee Act 1930 (a small estate)
      `account filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the executor or administrator has filed their account of the estate with the Registrar after the closing day
GIVETH A `What is left of my claim`
`Part VII -- what is left of my claim after the closing day` `advertised` `sent by the closing day` `executor otherwise knew` `where I lived` `notice published where I lived` `small estate` `account filed` MEANS
    IF NOT `advertised`
    THEN `Part VII does not touch your claim: the executor or administrator has not advertised for claims (NOTHING-kind); you may take the proceedings you otherwise could (s 57)`
    ELSE IF `sent by the closing day` OR `executor otherwise knew`
         THEN `your claim is kept: the executor or administrator must satisfy it, or keep back enough to satisfy it, before distributing (s 55), and is not released from it when the account is filed (s 56(3))`
         ELSE IF (NOT (`where I lived` EQUALS `in Tasmania`)) AND (NOT `notice published where I lived`) AND (NOT `small estate`)
              THEN `your claim is kept against the executor or administrator: you lived outside Tasmania and the notice was not published where s 54(4) requires, so the s 56(3) release does not reach you`
              ELSE IF `account filed`
                   THEN `the executor or administrator is released from your claim as to the assets paid or distributed (s 56(3)); you may still follow those assets into the hands of the beneficiaries or next of kin who received them (s 60), and assets not yet distributed still answer your claim (s 57)`
                   ELSE `the executor or administrator may now distribute without regard to your claim (s 55), and will be released as to what is distributed once the account is filed (s 56(3)); you may still follow assets into the hands of the beneficiaries or next of kin who received them (s 60)`

§§ `s 65 -- A will kept back or hidden`

-- Situation: only arises if someone kept back or hid a will.
-- s 65: "If any person retains or conceals, or is privy to the retention or concealment of, a will with intent to defraud
-- any person, the person defrauded and any person claiming under him, shall have an action for damages against such
-- first-mentioned person for any loss sustained by reason of such retention or concealment." The "if" failing is the
-- text's no ("this section gives no action on these facts"). The damages are the loss, assessed by a court.

@export Can I sue a person who kept back or hid a will, to cheat me (or the person I claim through) (Administration and Probate Act 1935 (Tas) s 65)? Yes: you have an action for damages for any loss the keeping back or hiding caused you. FALSE means this section gives no action on these facts.
GIVEN `kept back or hid the will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person kept back (retained) or hid (concealed) a will, or was a party to someone else doing so
      `to defraud me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did it with intent to defraud you, or the person you claim through
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 65 -- can I sue the person who hid the will` `kept back or hid the will` `to defraud me` IF
        `kept back or hid the will`
    AND `to defraud me`
